# The gateway's production command line. This file is the source of truth.
#
# Installed to /userdata/gateway.env by scripts/install_autostart.ps1 and read
# there by gateway-supervise.sh. Change it here and reinstall -- not on the
# board, where the next reinstall silently puts this file back.
#
#   apply    powershell -File scripts/install_autostart.ps1
#            adb shell "/etc/init.d/S99gateway restart"
#   revert   adb shell "cp /userdata/gateway.env.bak /userdata/gateway.env"
#
# Not used by scripts/verify-imu.sh or scripts/fps-osd-compare.sh: those encode
# to a file on purpose, because they want the frames back to look at.

GATEWAY_ARGS="-d /dev/video11 -w 1280 -H 720 --warmup 30 --sink rtsp --rtsp-port 8554 --threads --ring-slots 4 --quiet --osd --osd-source mpu6050"

# --osd-source mpu6050
#
# The overlay draws the real MPU6500's attitude onto every frame, read over
# software bit-banged I2C on gpio70/71. Measured on this board, 2026-09-29:
# thirty minutes and 54000 frames to RTSP at 30.000 fps with nothing dropped,
# nothing busy, and both pins released at the end.
#
# Do not switch this to mock "for safety". Mock is invented-looking attitude;
# a panel showing numbers nobody measured is worse than no panel.
#
# If the part does not answer, the encoder prints
#
#     OSD: IMU unavailable ... continuing without the overlay
#
# and streams anyway. That is deliberate: boot is the worst moment for a
# peripheral -- this one draws power alongside the camera while the 3A server
# is still converging -- and losing the video stream because the IMU was slow
# is worse than losing the panel.
#
# To check which happened after a boot:
#     adb shell "grep -E 'IMU attached|IMU unavailable' /userdata/gateway.log"
#
# --osd-imu-interval-ms
#
# Left unset, so it defaults to 100 ms. One bit-banged burst costs about 28 ms
# here -- measured, not estimated: an earlier comment called it "a few
# milliseconds", which was wrong by ten, and the frame rate paid for it. Same
# board, same session:
#
#     100 ms   29.998 fps    dropped busy    0    <- what this file uses
#      50 ms   26.612 fps                   38
#      10 ms   21.409 fps                  122
#
# Each row also carries what the encoder needs to do to a frame (about 12 ms):
# at 30 fps the budget is 33 ms, so a 28 ms burst every frame does not fit,
# while one every 100 ms costs 9 ms amortised and does. Lower it only after
# measuring again on this bus. It is not a round number; it is where the
# knee happened to be.
